{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93ce12203e3933f6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9016\n3\tepic_count: 2\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-9016 Decomposition: Idle resource reporting in the web console\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Idle and stale resource reports in the RHOAI web console | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Documentation for idle resource reports and safe reclamation | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P2 |\n18\t\n19\t## Dependency DAG\n20\t\n21\t```mermaid\n22\tgraph TD\n23\t    E001[\"E001: Idle and stale resource reports in the console (Implementation, P1)\"]\n24\t    E002[\"E002: Documentation for reports and safe reclamation (docs-authoring, P2)\"]\n25\t    E001 --> E002\n26\t```\n27\t\n28\t## DAG Justification\n29\t\n30\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n31\t|------|------|-----------|\n32\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | The documentation describes the reports that E001 builds; it must be authored against the implemented feature, not the plan. Per the docs-authoring priority exception, E002 stays P2 (strategy frontmatter priority Normal → P2) and does not push priority onto E001. |\n33\t\n34\t### Non-edges (parallel work) and structure decisions\n35\t\n36\t- **Single implementation epic for both reports (HLR-1 + HLR-2 + bundled HLR-3)**: both reports live in the same component (Dashboard), same team (RHOAI Dashboard), and follow the same architectural pattern — the console reads component backends via Kubernetes CRs and renders per-project resource lists (corroborated by `architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`). They share one logical report surface (per-project idle/stale resource reporting), so splitting them would force two epics to build the same UI surface and data aggregation pattern independently — a boundary violation. Estimated combined effort is within the ~2-week epic guideline.\n37\t- **No Investigation epics**: the strategy declares \"Open Questions: None\" and contains no risks table, pending reviews, or conditional ADRs. Whether the notebook controller's per-kernel activity data and KFP run metadata are exposed in exactly the form the console will consume is API-surface discovery — an implementation detail the team resolves at start of work, captured as an acceptance criterion and a health warning, not a genuine unknown that changes which epics exist.\n38\t- **No onboarding chain**: Dashboard is an existing, Konflux-onboarded GA component (`dashboard.md`); no `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation`, or `konflux-onboarding` epics are warranted.\n39\t- **Passive components (Notebooks, Data Science Pipelines) generate no epics**: the strategy states no code changes are needed there — the notebook controller already tracks last-activity timestamps and Kubeflow Pipelines retains run completion metadata, claims corroborated by `architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/notebooks.md` and `data-science-pipelines.md`. Their continued correct operation is validated as acceptance criteria on E001. The strategy names no cross-team validation owner, so no separate validation epics are generated.\n40\t\n41\t## Component Mapping\n42\t\n43\t| Strategy component | Canonical Jira component | Notes |\n44\t|--------------------|--------------------------|-------|\n45\t| RHOAI web console | Dashboard | Active component; all code changes land here |\n46\t| Notebook controller | Notebooks | Passive (no code changes); data source for HLR-1 |\n47\t| Kubeflow Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | Passive (no code changes); data source for HLR-2 |\n48\t| (documentation work) | Documentation | Work-product component for HLR-4; no architecture-context file exists for it (expected — it is not a platform component) |\n49\t\n50\tTeams were inferred from architecture-context owning-team fields (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard) because the strategy has no Impacted Teams table.\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n53\t\n54\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic | Notes |\n55\t|-----|----------|------------------------|------|-------|\n56\t| HLR-1 | P1 | Per-project idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources) from notebook controller activity tracking | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Primary P1 scope |\n57\t| HLR-2 | P1 | Stale pipeline-run report (completed runs older than configurable age, retained artifacts) from KFP run metadata | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Same component/team and same report surface as HLR-1 — one logical change, not split |\n58\t| HLR-3 | P2 | CSV export of either report | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 (bundled) | Bundled: CSV export shares the same report UI surfaces and data structures as the P1 reports — it is a presentation mode of the same report surface, not an independently deferrable feature. Splitting it would require a second pass over the same report views and data aggregation code. |\n59\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation explains the reports and safe reclamation steps | RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Substantive content authoring (new doc topic) — distinct work stream, separate docs-authoring epic |\n60\t\n61\t## Health Warnings\n62\t\n63\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture context; acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. Low risk for a scope this small, but the strategy is thinner than the refinement template suggests.\n64\t- **Scope trap / data-exposure risk**: `notebooks.md` states last activity is tracked *per kernel through Jupyter server API probes* (culler internals). Whether that timestamp is exposed to the console in a consumable form (e.g., on the Notebook CR) is unverified. If it is not, additional Notebooks-component work (exposing the activity timestamp) would be required — the strategy flags no open question here, so it is treated as an implementation detail (AC + risk on E001), but the implementing team should confirm early.\n65\t- **\"Single report\" wording vs. two HLRs**: the problem statement asks for \"a single report\" while HLR-1/HLR-2 describe two report views. Decomposed as one feature with two views in one epic; if the teams later prefer separate surfaces, the epic can be re-split along the workbench/pipeline seam without changing the DAG.\n66\t- **No priority inversions detected**: P1 reports before P2 export/docs is consistent with complexity and user value.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fb8b9fb617983ad","agent_id":"a0996fd62631ebff7","session_id":"e029b94f-474c-45be-acc9-edc4977b567a","uuid":"076abf69-71de-4304-a54a-f049daa396c3","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:14:54.547Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-93ce12203e3933f6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9016 decomposition"}
